WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.372
Downloaded model files should be treated as supply chain artifacts.

00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:11.028
Provenance matters because unknown sources can create trust, licensing, integrity, or safety concerns.

00:00:11.028 --> 00:00:16.117
Prefer model files from reputable repositories, maintainers, or organizations.

00:00:16.117 --> 00:00:19.967
Read model cards and release notes when they are available.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:27.471
Understand whether the file is an official release, a community conversion, a fine-tune, or an unknown repackaging.

00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:33.148
File integrity checks help confirm that the file you have matches the expected version.

00:00:33.148 --> 00:00:42.805
Hashes and checksums do not prove that a model is good or safe, but they can detect accidental corruption or unexpected modification after download.

00:00:42.805 --> 00:00:47.634
When a trusted source publishes hashes, record them with your local notes.

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:53.311
This is especially useful when moving model files between machines or rebuilding a lab.

00:00:53.311 --> 00:00:55.334
Licensing needs real attention.

00:00:55.334 --> 00:01:02.838
Model licenses may affect use, redistribution, modification, commercial deployment, attribution, or acceptable use.

00:01:02.838 --> 00:01:09.624
A model that is fine for personal experimentation may not be allowed in a client environment or product.

00:01:09.624 --> 00:01:12.952
Local execution does not erase license obligations.

00:01:12.952 --> 00:01:19.216
Reviewing the license before building workflows around a model prevents painful surprises later.

00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:26.002
Local inference can improve privacy because prompts and outputs do not automatically leave your machine.

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:29.199
That benefit depends on the surrounding workflow.

00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:36.246
Logs, dashboards, synced folders, screenshots, notebooks, and shared drives can still expose sensitive data.

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:44.533
Treat models, prompts, outputs, and runtime configuration with the same care you would apply to any other data-handling system.
